\DOC X86_MACRO_SIM_ABBREV_TAC

\TYPE {X86_MACRO_SIM_ABBREV_TAC : thm -> int -> (string * (term list)) list -> term -> tactic -> int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Discharges one inlined macro call inside an {eventually}-style x86 simulation
goal by proving the macro's local specification, advancing the symbolic state
to a new state variable and introducing fresh abbreviation variables for the
macro's results.

\DESCRIBE
The tactic is applied to an x86 simulation goal of the form
{A ?- eventually ... s}. Its arguments are: {mc : thm}, the (execution-trimmed)
machine-code theorem for the program being simulated; {codelen : int}, the
number of instructions the macro occupies, used to step past it; {localvars :
(string * term list) list}, the table mapping local-variable names to their
base pointer and offset, used to instantiate the macro's address arguments;
{template : term}, the parametrized specification (an {ensures} statement,
usually under several {!}-quantified parameters and hypotheses) that the
macro's code is shown to satisfy; {core_tac : tactic}, the tactic proving that
specification; {prep : int}, a count of preparatory instructions executed
before the macro body proper; and {ilist : string list}, the names of the local
variables involved, the first naming the output, to be abbreviated by a fresh
variable of that name, and the remainder naming the inputs.

The conclusion keeps its {eventually} body unchanged, with only its final state
argument replaced by a fresh later state variable. In the assumptions, every
fact mentioning the old state (program-counter value, register and memory
reads, flags, the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation from the initial state, and
the {bytes_loaded} fact) is removed and re-asserted for the new state, with the
program counter advanced past the macro, the {MAYCHANGE} relation extended by
the locations the macro may write, and the memory read for the output location
equated to a fresh variable {t} named by the first entry of {ilist}; an
assumption stating the macro's postcondition for {t} (often as an implication
guarded by the macro's input side conditions) is also added. All assumptions
not mentioning the state are retained.
{
       A ?- eventually ... s
     ========================================  X86_MACRO_SIM_ABBREV_TAC mc codelen localvars template core_tac prep ilist
       A' ?- eventually ... s'
}
\noindent where {A'} is {A} with the state-dependent facts re-stated at {s'},
plus the new abbreviation and postcondition assumptions.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of outcome, always on x86
simulation goals of this shape, and always leaving exactly one goal.

\ENDDOC
